cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this study, model, or code, please cite it as below. The study is PRELIMINARY until protocol/definition-of-finished gates pass; check CHANGELOG.md and results/manifests/ for the analysis version."
type: software
title: "Telo feasibility study: distributed sterile-injectable manufacturing under real-world constraints"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-09-01
authors:
  - family-names: Kavuru
    given-names: Rikhin
    email: rikhin@virahacks.com
repository-code: https://github.com/rikhinkavuru/Telo
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - drug shortages
  - sterile injectables
  - distributed manufacturing
  - supply-chain resilience
  - discrete-time simulation
  - feasibility study
abstract: >
  A pre-specified, falsifiable computational feasibility study asking under
  what product, demand, regulatory, and operating conditions distributed
  regional capacity can reduce sterile-injectable shortages more
  cost-effectively than additional safety stock, dual sourcing, reserved
  contract capacity, or additional centralized capacity. The study is
  designed to permit a negative conclusion.
